# ================================================================
# Convenience: bench(name, fn_ptr, iterations)
# ================================================================

fn bench(name, fp, n) {
    var b = bench_new(name);
    bench_run(b, fp, n);
    bench_report(b);
    return 0;
}

# Explicit batching: ONE clock pair per round of `batch` calls, instead of one
# per call.
#
# ⚠ THE REASON THIS COMMENT USED TO GIVE WAS A NUMBER, AND THE NUMBER WAS WRONG.
# It read "bench_run wraps a clock_gettime PAIR around every call, which the bench
# module documents at ~240 ns of overhead per op". That figure came from
# lib/bench.cyr's own header, where cyrius 6.5.19 RETIRED it: a clock read costs
# 15-32 ns on macOS arm64 and ~3,550 ns on aarch64 Linux, a 230x spread, and
# ~1,349 ns on this host — so no single number belongs in a comment at all. The
# module now calibrates the floor at runtime and subtracts it; read
# `bench_clock_overhead_ns()` if you need it.
# [measured: CHANGELOG.md 2.11.2 and lib/bench.cyr's four-host table; this host's floor: benchmarks.md, 2026-08-21]
#
# Since 6.5.19 `bench_run` sizes its own batches, so this helper is no longer
# needed to escape the floor — keep it only where a FIXED window matters (two
# runs compared at identical batch sizes). [measured: benchmarks.md, 2026-08-21]
#
# ⚠ And since cyrius 6.6.9 a fixed window decides whether the row reports a min and
# max at all: when batch x the row's mean cost is under 100 x (clock read + tick),
# lib/bench.cyr prints the mean as both, and the `[per op in ps ...]` line under the
# row says UNRESOLVED. On an hpet boot a sub-100 ns row in a 2,000-op window is
# under that bar. 3.3.6 decided the windows by a same-boot A/B (each row at its old
# and at a clearing window, alternating A B A B, three runs): 27 of the 40 call sites
# already resolve and keep 2000 x 200; the 13 that did not now run a window sized at
# 4x the bar with at least the old 400,000 ops. Their averages moved by -2.0% to +1.7%
# (five by more than their own spread, all faster), so those five rows' bench-history.csv
# trends carry a step of up to 2% at 3.3.6 that is the window, not the code. A re-run on
# the release tree widened ray_sphere too (its repair got cheaper: 64 ns, -1.2%); m4_mul
# cleared in all three runs there but sits within 5% of the bar, so a boot with a coarser
# clock tick can print it unresolved. Rows added
# from 3.3.6 on use `bench`, which sizes its chunks for the bar itself.
# [measured: CHANGELOG.md 3.3.6, window A/B: 3 same-boot runs, 2026-10-11 (floor 1.35-1.40 us, tick 977 ns)]
fn bench_batch(name, fp, batch, rounds) {
    var b = bench_new(name);
    bench_run_batch(b, fp, batch, rounds);
    bench_report(b);
    return 0;
}

