# ================================================================
# CGA (2.24.0) -- the module that had NO benchmark row at all
# ================================================================
# ⛔ Through 2.23.0 there was no CGA row among the 74, and that is exactly how a
# **2.7 ms first call** shipped in 2.21.0 and stayed invisible for three releases.
# Every CGA figure in that arc came from a throwaway probe, so none is in
# `bench-history.csv` and none would ever have been checked again.
# ⚠ THE COLD ROW IS THE POINT, AND IT IS THE HARD ONE. The null-basis product
# table is built LAZILY at first use, so **a benchmark that warms up cannot see
# the build cost** -- which is the whole reason the 2.7 ms hid. `bench(name, fp, 1)`
# works because `bench_run` clamps its pilot chunk to n, so n = 1 really is one
# shot. ⚠ An `alloc_used()` bound in a .tcyr was considered and rejected: it is
# deterministic, but 2.21.0's regression was CPU work rather than allocation, so
# it would NOT have caught the thing this row exists for -- and a wall-clock
# ceiling in a test is a flaky-test generator on a shared runner.
# ⚠ `_CGA_NULL_TBL` is checked so a cold row that is not actually cold cannot pass
# silently: warm, this reads ~400x faster and would look like a spectacular
# improvement rather than a broken measurement.
# [measured: cold 203.9 us vs warm 1.2 us; guard mutation-proven to exit 1, 2026-09-11]
var _b_cga_p = 0;
var _b_cga_q = 0;
var _b_cga_k5 = 0;
var _b_cga_k32 = 0;

